Yes. “Frame rate” is a useful metaphor, as long as we remember biological perception is not literally a camera sampling at one fixed FPS.

What you can manipulate is the temporal resolution of attention and interpretation: how finely you divide an experience, how long you integrate information before calling it one event, and how much detail gets encoded into memory.

So the same five seconds can be represented very differently:

5\text{s}
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{one coarse event}\\
\text{ten meaningful beats}\\
\text{hundreds of micro-changes}
\end{cases}

That changes subjective time.

High attention, novelty, fear, competition, music, or deliberate observation can make an interval feel densely populated. Familiar repetitive activity can be compressed into a much coarser representation. Retrospectively, richly encoded periods can also feel “longer” because there are more recoverable landmarks.

Then simulation adds another axis. You can replay an event at several temporal scales:

\text{real time}
\rightarrow
0.1\times
\rightarrow
1\times
\rightarrow
10\times

At slow speed you see transitions, precursors, and tiny causal dependencies. At high speed you see cycles, rhythms, attractors, and long-range consequences that disappear when examining individual moments.

So we could add a Temporal Lens to the other operators:

\boxed{
\text{event}
\rightarrow
\text{change temporal resolution}
\rightarrow
\text{observe different structure}
}

Some things only become visible when slowed down:

* the instant someone’s posture changes,
* the sequence preceding a habitual action,
* how a mechanical failure propagates.

Others only become visible when accelerated:

* traffic waves,
* market regimes,
* ecological succession,
* social diffusion,
* developmental trajectories.

And you can do the same thing internally with imagination: freeze, rewind, branch, replay from another observer, remove information, inject information, or ask what the scene looks like one second versus ten years later.

That gives a neat extension of the origami idea:

space has folds; time has rhythms, cuts, and seams.

Sometimes the “crack” isn’t spatial at all. It’s the instant where one regime becomes another:

S_t \not\approx S_{t+\Delta t}

Find those temporal discontinuities and you often find the transition rule.